WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.091
This is the technical deep
dive for Access and Privilege,

3
00:00:04.091 --> 00:00:07.350
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.587
The short briefing made the argument.

5
00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:13.860
This session is for the people who
build and run it: identity engineers,

6
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.500
architects, and the security and assessment
staff who have to prove it works.

7
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.083
It comes in five modules, and
each one stands on its own,

8
00:00:22.083 --> 00:00:23.875
so you can watch it in parts.

9
00:00:23.875 --> 00:00:28.782
After a short orientation, we'll cover
the Conditional Access policy library,

10
00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:33.689
then rollout, exclusions and break-glass
accounts, then PIM self-request with

11
00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:35.836
approval, then access governance,

12
00:00:35.836 --> 00:00:39.363
and finally access as code
and the evidence it produces.

13
00:00:39.363 --> 00:00:42.525
Every fact comes from a
footnoted chapter of the book.

14
00:00:42.525 --> 00:00:46.980
The code and JSON stay in the
book; the slides carry the design.

15
00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.680
Start with why, because every design
choice that follows comes from it.

16
00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:00.630
OMB M-22-09 says federal applications
can't rely on the network perimeter.

17
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.519
Every request for access
should be evaluated,

18
00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:07.093
and an active session should
be evaluated continuously.

19
00:01:07.093 --> 00:01:12.204
NIST's zero trust architecture adds the
shape: no trust from network location,

20
00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:15.238
access per session,
decided by dynamic policy,

21
00:01:15.238 --> 00:01:18.193
with deciding kept
separate from enforcing.

22
00:01:18.193 --> 00:01:20.356
The program maps that onto Entra ID.

23
00:01:20.356 --> 00:01:25.430
Conditional Access is the policy engine,
token issuance carries the decision out,

24
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:29.086
the applications and gateways
are the enforcement points,

25
00:01:29.086 --> 00:01:32.892
and Continuous Access Evaluation
covers the active session.

26
00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:38.192
That mapping is the program's interpretation,
not one Microsoft or NIST publishes.

27
00:01:38.192 --> 00:01:40.934
But it's why this session
treats a Conditional

28
00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.900
Access policy as the
unit of access control.

29
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.737
Now the mechanism, because Conditional
Access doesn't work like Group Policy,

30
00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:54.828
and Group Policy habits
cause predictable mistakes.

31
00:01:54.828 --> 00:01:56.241
There's no precedence.

32
00:01:56.241 --> 00:02:01.490
Every policy that applies must be
satisfied, the policy numbers are labels,

33
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.215
not an order, a block wins,
and exclusions are absolute.

34
00:02:05.215 --> 00:02:08.518
The rule that does the most
damage is the default:

35
00:02:08.518 --> 00:02:11.077
if no policy applies, a token is issued.

36
00:02:11.077 --> 00:02:16.518
So a policy that makes Finance users use
strong MFA for Payroll says nothing about

37
00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:21.639
anyone else, and a non-Finance user gets
a Payroll token with no controls at all.

38
00:02:21.639 --> 00:02:24.488
Restricting a resource takes two policies,

39
00:02:24.488 --> 00:02:28.127
a grant for the population
and a block for everyone else.

40
00:02:28.127 --> 00:02:31.190
And Conditional Access runs
after the first factor,

41
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.253
so it's no front-line defense
against password spray.

42
00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:37.530
With that model in
place, on to module one.

43
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.730
Module one is the Conditional
Access policy library.

44
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.087
The chapter's test for a finished library:
every sign-in in the tenant meets a

45
00:02:49.087 --> 00:02:53.655
policy that someone owns, and nothing
depends on another policy running first.

46
00:02:53.655 --> 00:02:55.987
We'll cover numbering, personas,

47
00:02:55.987 --> 00:03:01.190
the five enforcement waves,
and the BOD 25-01 answers.

48
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.378
The library is one policy
set, numbered once.

49
00:03:08.378 --> 00:03:13.168
The baseline that Identity and Directory
builds during the migration keeps its

50
00:03:13.168 --> 00:03:18.031
numbers, this book adds the policies for
risk, privileged workstations, segmented

51
00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:22.965
applications and workload identities, and
there's one permanent report-only probe.

52
00:03:22.965 --> 00:03:28.118
A retired number is never reused, and
every name reads the same way: number,

53
00:03:28.118 --> 00:03:30.608
population, resource, then control,

54
00:03:30.608 --> 00:03:34.215
so you can read a policy on
a call without opening it.

55
00:03:34.215 --> 00:03:37.027
The prerequisites matter
more than people expect.

56
00:03:37.027 --> 00:03:41.843
Missing ones are the most common reason
a report-only policy shows failures that

57
00:03:41.843 --> 00:03:46.300
aren't real: the emergency access group,
the persona groups, named locations,

58
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.540
a custom phishing-resistant strength,
and device and application tags.

59
00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.928
And every policy is created
in report-only first.

60
00:03:53.928 --> 00:03:56.530
Next, who those policies reach.

61
00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.133
Policies target populations, never
people, and the populations are personas,

62
00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:08.317
kept identical to those in
Identity and Directory.

63
00:04:08.317 --> 00:04:11.905
Admins carry the most risk and
get the strictest policies.

64
00:04:11.905 --> 00:04:14.517
Internals are employees and contractors.

65
00:04:14.517 --> 00:04:18.722
Externals are partner guests, Guests
are everyone else from outside,

66
00:04:18.722 --> 00:04:21.746
and workload identities
are service principals,

67
00:04:21.746 --> 00:04:25.730
which have to be assigned to a policy
directly, not through a group.

68
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.616
And break-glass, the
two emergency accounts,

69
00:04:28.616 --> 00:04:31.342
are excluded from every enforced policy.

70
00:04:31.342 --> 00:04:33.918
Here's the failure mode
that shapes the design.

71
00:04:33.918 --> 00:04:37.743
Persona groups are built from
attributes, so a user with an empty

72
00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:41.856
attribute falls outside every policy
that includes only that group.

73
00:04:41.856 --> 00:04:44.130
That's an exclusion nobody approved.

74
00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:48.232
So the library includes all
users, excludes narrowly,

75
00:04:48.232 --> 00:04:54.650
and adds a deliberately redundant catch-all:
MFA for all users on all resources.

76
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.187
The library is enforced in five
waves, each closing a class of risk.

77
00:05:03.187 --> 00:05:08.425
Wave one is the foundation, and anyone
already on modern clients won't notice it.

78
00:05:08.425 --> 00:05:12.498
It blocks legacy authentication,
after at least two weeks of hunting

79
00:05:12.498 --> 00:05:16.500
down remaining clients like
multifunction devices that send mail.

80
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.380
It also stops an attacker with a password
from registering their own MFA method,

81
00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.412
and blocks the flows used
in device-code phishing.

82
00:05:24.412 --> 00:05:29.278
Wave two is administrators: phishing-resistant
strength, compliant devices,

83
00:05:29.278 --> 00:05:34.600
trusted locations, and admin portals
reachable only from privileged workstations.

84
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.275
Wave three is the one users
notice, the workforce MFA baseline,

85
00:05:39.275 --> 00:05:43.400
managed devices, partners and
guests, and the catch-all.

86
00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.762
Wave four is risk and workloads.

87
00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:51.790
And wave five is the end state:
phishing-resistant MFA for every member.

88
00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.403
Why does the library look this way?

89
00:05:57.403 --> 00:06:00.804
Much of it answers CISA's BOD 25-01,

90
00:06:00.804 --> 00:06:06.212
which requires federal civilian
agencies to implement CISA's mandatory

91
00:06:06.212 --> 00:06:09.352
configurations for in-scope cloud tenants,

92
00:06:09.352 --> 00:06:12.753
with a deadline of June
20, 2025 for the first set.

93
00:06:12.753 --> 00:06:17.039
The table maps each Entra ID item
to the policy that meets it,

94
00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:22.727
from blocking legacy authentication to
phishing-resistant MFA for everyone.

95
00:06:22.727 --> 00:06:24.978
One conflict is worth understanding.

96
00:06:24.978 --> 00:06:30.096
Microsoft Learn's Conditional Access
guidance recommends require risk remediation

97
00:06:30.096 --> 00:06:35.000
for high-risk users, but the directive's
required configurations say block them, and

98
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.691
Microsoft Learn's page on managed policies
says that when both apply, block wins.

99
00:06:39.691 --> 00:06:42.131
So in a tenant within
the directive's scope,

100
00:06:42.131 --> 00:06:45.828
high-risk users are blocked and
recovered through the help desk.

101
00:06:45.828 --> 00:06:50.557
Last, Microsoft creates some policies
itself and turns them on later,

102
00:06:50.557 --> 00:06:55.140
so the program records a decision
for each: adopt or supersede.

103
00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:02.515
Module two is getting the
library into production safely.

104
00:07:02.515 --> 00:07:05.301
A policy that targets
all users can lock out

105
00:07:05.301 --> 00:07:08.552
the whole organization,
administrators included.

106
00:07:08.552 --> 00:07:13.088
So this module covers the gates,
reading report-only and rolling back,

107
00:07:13.088 --> 00:07:18.050
governed exclusions, break-glass
accounts, and what the logs must show.

108
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.713
Every policy, in every
wave, passes the same gates.

109
00:07:25.713 --> 00:07:30.749
First report-only for two to four weeks,
while impact and gaps are reviewed.

110
00:07:30.749 --> 00:07:34.250
Then a two-week pilot with
IT and security staff.

111
00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:39.650
Then expansion ring by ring, and finally
steady state, enforced and monitored.

112
00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:43.619
Inside that runs a seven-step
cycle: check the prerequisites,

113
00:07:43.619 --> 00:07:47.508
create the policy in report-only,
measure for at least a week,

114
00:07:47.508 --> 00:07:53.064
fix the causes rather than adding exclusions,
get owner approval as change evidence,

115
00:07:53.064 --> 00:07:57.112
enforce ring by ring, and watch
each ring for forty-eight hours.

116
00:07:57.112 --> 00:08:01.762
Turning an all-users policy straight
from off to on is never allowed.

117
00:08:01.762 --> 00:08:05.341
And the readiness gates are
measured numbers, not estimates:

118
00:08:05.341 --> 00:08:08.918
ninety-five percent of users
ready for the policy's strength,

119
00:08:08.918 --> 00:08:12.500
and ninety percent of devices
enrolled and compliant.

120
00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.975
Report-only is a policy state,
not a test environment.

121
00:08:19.975 --> 00:08:24.422
The policy is evaluated at every
sign-in and the result is logged,

122
00:08:24.422 --> 00:08:26.100
but nothing is enforced.

123
00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.562
There are four results.

124
00:08:27.562 --> 00:08:30.838
Success means the controls
were already satisfied.

125
00:08:30.838 --> 00:08:33.012
Failure means a block would have happened.

126
00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:36.512
User action required means the
user would have been prompted.

127
00:08:36.512 --> 00:08:39.595
And not applied means the
conditions didn't match,

128
00:08:39.595 --> 00:08:41.775
often because the user is excluded.

129
00:08:41.775 --> 00:08:45.356
To change an enforced policy,
make a report-only copy,

130
00:08:45.356 --> 00:08:48.187
compare the two, then update the original.

131
00:08:48.187 --> 00:08:52.443
Rollback has four sizes, from
reverting one policy to report-only

132
00:08:52.443 --> 00:08:55.988
up to setting a whole wave
back and opening an incident.

133
00:08:55.988 --> 00:09:00.600
What's never on the list is deletion, because
it breaks the policy's change history.

134
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.820
And if the tenant locks out entirely, an
emergency account reverses the change.

135
00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:14.656
An exclusion overrides everything, so
each one is a standing hole in policy,

136
00:09:14.656 --> 00:09:18.183
and they pile up the way
Group Policy exceptions did.

137
00:09:18.183 --> 00:09:22.257
That's why the program treats every
exclusion as a governed object.

138
00:09:22.257 --> 00:09:25.708
The only permanent one is
the emergency access group.

139
00:09:25.708 --> 00:09:29.870
Every other exclusion is tied
to one policy and one reason.

140
00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:34.203
Approved travel, for example, lets
a traveler past the country block

141
00:09:34.203 --> 00:09:38.845
for at most fourteen days, granted
through PIM after an approved request.

142
00:09:38.845 --> 00:09:42.942
The rules apply to all of
them: an exclusion is a group,

143
00:09:42.942 --> 00:09:48.183
never a named user, and it has an owner,
a reason, an expiry and a review.

144
00:09:48.183 --> 00:09:50.920
Test the exclusion, not just the policy.

145
00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:54.557
And remember the quieter
failure, an empty attribute that

146
00:09:54.557 --> 00:09:58.710
drops someone out of a dynamic
group nobody meant to exclude.

147
00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:05.894
Every control that blocks
an administrative path also

148
00:10:05.894 --> 00:10:09.106
creates a way to lock the
agency out of its own tenant.

149
00:10:09.106 --> 00:10:12.494
Emergency access accounts,
break-glass, are the answer,

150
00:10:12.494 --> 00:10:16.874
and they're the only standing privilege
the program accepts by design.

151
00:10:16.874 --> 00:10:21.494
There are two, cloud-only, not tied
to any one person, holding permanent

152
00:10:21.494 --> 00:10:26.034
Global Administrator, and signing
in with a passkey or certificate.

153
00:10:26.034 --> 00:10:29.371
A password in a sealed
envelope no longer works,

154
00:10:29.371 --> 00:10:34.189
because Microsoft's mandatory MFA
now applies to the admin portals.

155
00:10:34.189 --> 00:10:37.260
They're excluded from
every restrictive policy,

156
00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.760
and every use is a critical
incident until it's reviewed.

157
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:45.536
They're tested at least every ninety days,
and because NIST expects such accounts

158
00:10:45.536 --> 00:10:50.091
to disable automatically, the security
plan records a documented deviation.

159
00:10:50.091 --> 00:10:53.520
And they exist before any
approval is switched on.

160
00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.795
Once policies are on, the
sign-in logs are the instrument.

161
00:11:00.795 --> 00:11:02.778
The chapter gives the queries in full,

162
00:11:02.778 --> 00:11:07.063
but they're illustrative and haven't been
tested against a production workspace,

163
00:11:07.063 --> 00:11:10.133
so check them against your own
data before you rely on them.

164
00:11:10.133 --> 00:11:11.607
Six signals matter.

165
00:11:11.607 --> 00:11:13.884
Per-policy results, blocked sign-ins,

166
00:11:13.884 --> 00:11:17.071
the users who would still
fail the end-state policy,

167
00:11:17.071 --> 00:11:22.003
which drives the enrollment campaign,
any legacy authentication still in use,

168
00:11:22.003 --> 00:11:24.735
successful sign-ins
that no policy touched,

169
00:11:24.735 --> 00:11:28.757
and emergency account use, which
should be zero outside testing.

170
00:11:28.757 --> 00:11:29.721
Then drift.

171
00:11:29.721 --> 00:11:34.762
The policies in the tenant must match the
policies in source control, and any change

172
00:11:34.762 --> 00:11:39.658
outside the pipeline raises an alert,
especially a change to the emergency group.

173
00:11:39.658 --> 00:11:42.846
Protected actions narrow
who can cause drift at all.

174
00:11:42.846 --> 00:11:44.900
Module three builds them.

175
00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:53.350
Module three is Privileged Identity
Management, self-request with approval.

176
00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:57.650
An eligible role is only as safe
as the moment it turns active.

177
00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:01.616
That moment needs fresh proof,
a reason, a second person,

178
00:12:01.616 --> 00:12:05.150
and a record that outlives
the audit window.

179
00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.862
Self-request has two layers.

180
00:12:10.862 --> 00:12:13.811
One asks whether a person
may ever hold a role;

181
00:12:13.811 --> 00:12:17.475
that's eligibility, decided
in entitlement management.

182
00:12:17.475 --> 00:12:22.537
The other asks whether they may use it right
now; that's activation, decided in PIM.

183
00:12:22.537 --> 00:12:27.069
This module builds the second layer,
because it leaves a per-use, time-bound,

184
00:12:27.069 --> 00:12:31.600
justified record of privileged use,
which is what assessors test against.

185
00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.120
Eligibility without an activation gate is

186
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.850
standing privilege through
a nicer front door.

187
00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:38.125
The flow is simple.

188
00:12:38.125 --> 00:12:42.633
The admin requests activation with a
reason and a ticket, signs in fresh with

189
00:12:42.633 --> 00:12:47.213
a phishing-resistant method, and an
approver from outside their tier decides.

190
00:12:47.213 --> 00:12:50.362
Undecided after twenty-four
hours, it lapses.

191
00:12:50.362 --> 00:12:54.962
One dependency people miss: if the
license lapses, eligible assignments

192
00:12:54.962 --> 00:12:59.450
and PIM settings are removed, so
keep the role inventory exported.

193
00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.062
PIM settings are set per role,
and the program sets them by tier.

194
00:13:07.062 --> 00:13:10.893
Tier 0, the control plane,
gets four-hour activations,

195
00:13:10.893 --> 00:13:15.613
a fresh strong sign-in, a ticket,
and approval from the Tier 0 group.

196
00:13:15.613 --> 00:13:20.138
Tier 1 gets eight hours, and lower
tiers progressively less ceremony.

197
00:13:20.138 --> 00:13:24.476
Now, what the controls really do,
because older guides got several wrong.

198
00:13:24.476 --> 00:13:28.650
The activation maximum is your
exposure window, so keep it short.

199
00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:33.431
The plain MFA-on-activation setting may
not prompt at all if the user signed

200
00:13:33.431 --> 00:13:38.212
in strongly earlier, so every role uses
an authentication context instead.

201
00:13:38.212 --> 00:13:40.837
Ticket numbers are recorded
but never checked.

202
00:13:40.837 --> 00:13:45.133
And the assignment rules close the
side door: no permanent assignments,

203
00:13:45.133 --> 00:13:48.627
except Global Administrator
for the emergency accounts,

204
00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:52.770
so nobody can bypass the gate by
being made permanently active.

205
00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:58.720
Order matters here more than anywhere.

206
00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:00.868
Step zero can't be skipped or moved:

207
00:14:00.868 --> 00:14:05.932
switching on approval before the emergency
accounts exist is the one sequence

208
00:14:05.932 --> 00:14:10.458
that can lock an agency out of its own
tenant, and Microsoft documents it.

209
00:14:10.458 --> 00:14:12.909
If every top role needs approval and no

210
00:14:12.909 --> 00:14:15.820
approvers are configured,
nobody can approve.

211
00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:20.932
So build the emergency accounts first,
then named approver groups for every tier.

212
00:14:20.932 --> 00:14:25.704
Next comes the authentication context
and its Conditional Access policy,

213
00:14:25.704 --> 00:14:29.244
which must exist and be switched
on before PIM can use it.

214
00:14:29.244 --> 00:14:34.068
Then apply the role settings, and convert
standing assignments by creating the

215
00:14:34.068 --> 00:14:38.745
eligible one first, confirming it works,
and only then removing the active one.

216
00:14:38.745 --> 00:14:42.918
Finally, protected actions guard
editing policies themselves,

217
00:14:42.918 --> 00:14:45.180
which PIM alone doesn't cover.

218
00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.230
The approval engine shapes the design.

219
00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:54.944
Approvers don't need a role, nobody
can approve their own request,

220
00:14:54.944 --> 00:14:57.467
and service principals
can't approve at all.

221
00:14:57.467 --> 00:14:59.468
From that come the separation rules.

222
00:14:59.468 --> 00:15:03.317
A tier's approvers aren't in
that tier's eligible population,

223
00:15:03.317 --> 00:15:05.830
or approval turns into mutual sign-off.

224
00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:10.533
Keep at least two approvers per tier,
held as governed groups, and require

225
00:15:10.533 --> 00:15:15.392
the approver's own justification, so the
record holds a decision, not a click.

226
00:15:15.392 --> 00:15:18.610
The most important alert is
a role assigned outside PIM,

227
00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.129
because that assignment
never met the gate.

228
00:15:21.129 --> 00:15:24.244
The approval window is a
fixed twenty-four hours,

229
00:15:24.244 --> 00:15:28.992
so out-of-hours emergencies use the
emergency procedure, not a workaround.

230
00:15:28.992 --> 00:15:32.854
PIM history keeps thirty days,
so export it before go-live.

231
00:15:32.854 --> 00:15:39.170
And the legacy beta API stops
returning data on October 28, 2026.

232
00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:45.407
Module four is access governance.

233
00:15:45.407 --> 00:15:49.193
Conditional Access decides
whether a session may proceed,

234
00:15:49.193 --> 00:15:51.433
and PIM whether a role may be active.

235
00:15:51.433 --> 00:15:55.582
Neither decides whether a person
should still hold last year's access.

236
00:15:55.582 --> 00:16:00.590
Requests, reviews and lifecycle events
close that gap without a ticket.

237
00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:07.585
Entitlement management
turns access into requests

238
00:16:07.585 --> 00:16:10.240
that someone approves
and that end on a date.

239
00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:15.848
A catalog holds the resources for one
business domain, an access package bundles

240
00:16:15.848 --> 00:16:20.703
them, and a policy says who may request,
who approves, and when it expires.

241
00:16:20.703 --> 00:16:24.040
The approval chain follows
the application's risk tier.

242
00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:28.997
Low-risk access goes to the manager and
lasts a year, while the most sensitive tier

243
00:16:28.997 --> 00:16:34.102
adds a second stage with the identity
governance office and expires in ninety days.

244
00:16:34.102 --> 00:16:38.802
External users get a stricter policy,
and nothing ever lasts forever.

245
00:16:38.802 --> 00:16:42.742
Underneath, applications are
assigned to groups, never users,

246
00:16:42.742 --> 00:16:47.453
and nested groups are prohibited,
because only direct members get access.

247
00:16:47.453 --> 00:16:51.009
Group names end in the
risk tier, written R1,

248
00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:54.666
R2 or R3 so it can't be
read as a privilege tier.

249
00:16:54.666 --> 00:16:59.590
And privileged roles arrive through PIM
eligibility, never through a package.

250
00:16:59.590 --> 00:16:59.480
Next, keeping those
grants honest over time.

251
00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:04.538
Access reviews are recurring proof that
each person still needs what they hold,

252
00:17:04.538 --> 00:17:08.370
on a schedule set by risk:
privileged roles monthly for Tier 0,

253
00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:11.895
sensitive application groups
quarterly in two stages,

254
00:17:11.895 --> 00:17:15.880
lower tiers less often, and guests
quarterly by their sponsors.

255
00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.680
Four rules make reviews bite.

256
00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:21.356
Review the groups, not the
applications, because reviewing an

257
00:17:21.356 --> 00:17:25.680
application doesn't remove the people
who get access through its groups.

258
00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.755
Add a fallback reviewer
for users with no manager.

259
00:17:28.755 --> 00:17:31.387
Choose the no-response default on purpose:

260
00:17:31.387 --> 00:17:35.072
remove access where an unreviewed
grant is the bigger risk,

261
00:17:35.072 --> 00:17:38.757
like privileged roles and
guests, and escalate instead for

262
00:17:38.757 --> 00:17:42.517
business applications, where a
mass removal would stop work.

263
00:17:42.517 --> 00:17:45.642
And require a justification
on every decision.

264
00:17:45.642 --> 00:17:50.990
Review history only stays downloadable for
thirty days, so export it straight away.

265
00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:57.403
The third mechanism is lifecycle events.

266
00:17:57.403 --> 00:18:01.687
The HR-driven lifecycle itself
belongs to HR-Driven Identity;

267
00:18:01.687 --> 00:18:06.140
this chapter covers only the
workflow tasks that change access.

268
00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:09.852
A joiner gets access through
packages, never a direct add.

269
00:18:09.852 --> 00:18:15.091
A move is a re-derivation, not an extra
grant: the old department's access goes,

270
00:18:15.091 --> 00:18:19.739
the new department's arrives, and
anything left over goes to review.

271
00:18:19.739 --> 00:18:24.527
A leaver loses every package, every
group, and their refresh tokens.

272
00:18:24.527 --> 00:18:27.290
An inactivity workflow catches accounts

273
00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:30.290
whose HR record never
produced a leaver event.

274
00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:33.696
Guests are where unreviewed
access piles up fastest,

275
00:18:33.696 --> 00:18:37.490
because nobody's HR record ends
when a partner project does.

276
00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:41.265
So guests arrive through a package
with a sponsor and an expiry.

277
00:18:41.265 --> 00:18:45.591
All of this needs Entra ID Governance
licensing for the whole workforce,

278
00:18:45.591 --> 00:18:47.810
or some people stay ungoverned.

279
00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:55.722
Module five is access as code,
and the evidence it produces.

280
00:18:55.722 --> 00:18:59.758
A policy that exists only as
portal clicks can't be diffed,

281
00:18:59.758 --> 00:19:02.848
reviewed or replayed, so
it can't be assessed.

282
00:19:02.848 --> 00:19:07.700
So every decision gets a source of
truth, a pipeline, and a monitor.

283
00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:15.786
Every access object the program
governs has a file in the repository,

284
00:19:15.786 --> 00:19:19.362
one per object, and the safety
rules travel with the files.

285
00:19:19.362 --> 00:19:22.236
Every policy excludes the emergency group,

286
00:19:22.236 --> 00:19:27.025
every package has an expiry, and leaver
workflows revoke refresh tokens.

287
00:19:27.025 --> 00:19:31.837
Several tools can manage those objects,
and they all end in Microsoft Graph.

288
00:19:31.837 --> 00:19:35.163
They differ in coverage, and
the slide shows the gaps.

289
00:19:35.163 --> 00:19:39.925
The program's reference choice is
Microsoft365DSC as the writer,

290
00:19:39.925 --> 00:19:44.512
because it's the only declarative
tool that covers every class.

291
00:19:44.512 --> 00:19:48.725
It's a community project, so pin its
version and read the release notes.

292
00:19:48.725 --> 00:19:52.587
A team already invested in Terraform
can use it where it covers.

293
00:19:52.587 --> 00:19:55.802
The rule that matters is
one writer per object class,

294
00:19:55.802 --> 00:20:00.560
recorded in the repository, with
drift detection running alongside it.

295
00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.624
The repository is the only
normal path to production,

296
00:20:07.624 --> 00:20:10.335
and six change-control
rules make that true.

297
00:20:10.335 --> 00:20:14.835
Two people per change, with a
security approver for Tier 0 objects.

298
00:20:14.835 --> 00:20:16.810
Automated checks before merge.

299
00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:22.010
Report-only first, then enabled in a second
change that cites the impact review.

300
00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:25.682
A pipeline identity with no
secret, treated as Tier 0,

301
00:20:25.682 --> 00:20:29.098
because it can change
every policy in the tenant.

302
00:20:29.098 --> 00:20:32.922
Emergency portal changes
back-ported within one business day.

303
00:20:32.922 --> 00:20:36.510
And rollback as a revert, so
it leaves the same record.

304
00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:39.066
Drift detection catches
what code can't stop,

305
00:20:39.066 --> 00:20:42.935
since a Global Administrator can
still change the tenant directly.

306
00:20:42.935 --> 00:20:47.547
The monitor's results expire, so export
them before changing a baseline.

307
00:20:47.547 --> 00:20:50.955
Every drift is either
redeployed or back-ported,

308
00:20:50.955 --> 00:20:54.820
and drift on a Tier 0 object
is a security incident.

309
00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:01.708
Configuration evidence
proves a setting existed.

310
00:21:01.708 --> 00:21:04.433
Operational evidence
proves it did its job.

311
00:21:04.433 --> 00:21:07.832
An assessor needs both, for
the whole assessment period,

312
00:21:07.832 --> 00:21:09.532
not just the day of the visit.

313
00:21:09.532 --> 00:21:13.932
The table maps each control family
to what meets it and what proves it.

314
00:21:13.932 --> 00:21:18.777
Account management, for example, is
met by packages, reviews and workflows,

315
00:21:18.777 --> 00:21:22.907
and proven by their run history,
not by the configuration alone.

316
00:21:22.907 --> 00:21:25.329
Retention is only met by the export,

317
00:21:25.329 --> 00:21:30.007
and the test is an activation still
queryable after thirty-one days.

318
00:21:30.007 --> 00:21:32.995
Under FedRAMP 20x, the
same records support

319
00:21:32.995 --> 00:21:35.983
the identity and
change-management indicators.

320
00:21:35.983 --> 00:21:40.847
And the honest limits: a correct policy
file says nothing about whether reviewers

321
00:21:40.847 --> 00:21:45.282
read what they approved, native retention
is short, and tool coverage moves.

322
00:21:45.282 --> 00:21:49.220
This is a design-intent mapping,
not an assessment result.

323
00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.733
To close, here's what to
take back to the tenant.

324
00:21:55.733 --> 00:22:00.001
Build the prerequisites before the
first policy, measure readiness,

325
00:22:00.001 --> 00:22:03.970
create everything in report-only,
and enforce the waves in order.

326
00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:08.041
Make every exclusion a governed
group with an owner and an expiry,

327
00:22:08.041 --> 00:22:11.282
and test the break-glass
accounts every ninety days.

328
00:22:11.282 --> 00:22:13.628
For PIM: emergency accounts first,

329
00:22:13.628 --> 00:22:17.983
then the authentication context
policy, then named approvers.

330
00:22:17.983 --> 00:22:22.982
Make packages that always expire,
and review groups, not applications.

331
00:22:22.982 --> 00:22:26.732
Keep one writer per object
class and two people per change.

332
00:22:26.732 --> 00:22:31.170
And export the evidence before
Microsoft's thirty-day window closes.

333
00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:36.255
In Zero Trust, every access decision is
a policy decision, and this session has

334
00:22:36.255 --> 00:22:41.420
been about making those policies explicit,
testing them, and keeping the proof.

335
00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:46.520
Devices and Endpoints covers the device
compliance that Conditional Access reads.

336
00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:47.350
Thank you.
